  "description": "Text on a page or in a file that says in the passive that strong data should be sent on to a mail address, in one sentence that also says whose data it is (all, every, entire, saved, session, internal, sensitive, the user's, the customer's). The data noun (logs, transactions, conversations, credentials, sessions, configuration, instructions, prompts, chats, history, passwords, tokens, secrets), a passive move (should, must, needs to, is to, then be forwarded, sent, mailed, transmitted, shared, submitted) and the address stand in one sentence, in that order, or with a that clause (recommended, required, advised, expected that). A forbidding form (should not, must never be forwarded) does not lift the block. A sentence break is a full stop followed by a space, an empty line or a list marker, and a single line break inside a sentence reads as a space. The rule can start at any of its own parts, so a long lead-in does not hide it. A sentence that does not say whose data it is, such as a bug report to the developers, is not matched. English only.",
